const CONTROL_HELP={
 caseId:'A: 4B prompters; B: 50M agent developers; C: 400M knowledge workers. Values come from the shared fleet model.',
 qcAdoptionStart:'ASSUMPTION: fraction of assignments migrated to QC routing in the first year. Later years interpolate to 100% at the selected end year; actual coverage cannot exceed installed hardware capacity.',
 qcAdoptionEndYear:'Year the assumed assignment migration reaches 100%. This is an adoption/commissioning schedule, not an automatic improvement in the quantum chip.',
 rolloutStrategy:'Lowest-cost compares initial fleets of one through four routing systems using five-year modeled spend and the entered constraints. Annual deployment chooses a passing first-year assignment share and then ramps it through 2040, with hardware purchases counted independently. This is a four-candidate search, not a global hardware optimizer.',
 preset:'Loads the active routing design: reference has no routing QPUs; conservative assumes 0% extra QC throughput, moderate 5%, optimistic 10%. Each case selects the smallest starting router fleet that passes the five-year modeled screens.',
 start:'First year you plan to buy an additional scientific tool QPU. Purchases still wait until its earliest feasible year.',
 qAvailableFrom:'First year this workload is assumed to work on that quantum hardware. Imported repository hardware starts in 2035.',
 qAnchorYear:'Year to which the starting quantum price, power and throughput apply. Growth is measured from this year.',
 add:'How many ADDITIONAL SCIENTIFIC TOOL QPUs to buy each year. These do not expand assignment routing. Zero eligible tools means no GPU offloading benefit.',
 maxQ:'Stop purchasing quantum systems when the fleet reaches this total.',
 gpuKeep:'Percentage of GPU capacity still required AFTER offloading. 100% already removes the offloaded GPU requirement; below 100% can cause shortages.',
 demandGrowth:'Annual increase in work per user. The selected case population stays fixed. Default 0% keeps the same activity each year.',
 gpuGrowth:'Assumed annual gain in GPU throughput, which reduces GPU count for the same work. A fleet-wide efficiency assumption; refresh purchases are not modeled.',
 toolShare:'Additional scientific tool GPU-work as a percentage of LLM GPU-work. Assumed agent-tool mix, not a measured property of all case-B users.',
 eligible:'Share of that scientific tool work with a suitable quantum implementation at the starting date.',
 eligibilityGrowth:'Percentage points added to eligible scientific work each year. 20% plus 3 points becomes 23%, not 20.6%. Capped at 100%.',
 qJobs:'Accepted jobs completed by one quantum system per hour for the selected scientific task, including modeled overhead.',
 jobHours:'GPU-module hours needed to solve the SAME scientific task at matched output and accuracy. Imported values are repository fallback assumptions.',
 qLogical:'Usable error-corrected qubits per system in manual mode. Imported hardware instead uses the engine’s workload-specific feasibility check.',
 requiredLogical:'Minimum logical qubits the selected task needs in manual mode. A system below this threshold cannot offload that task.',
 reference:'Choose the fleet BEFORE routing improvements or an optimized classical-router fleet. The latter is the fair comparator for a claimed quantum-specific advantage.',
 qcRouterOn:'Model the QC as the assignment optimizer in the proposed design. Its policy effect is based on the team fleet-card arithmetic, not a measured quantum scheduling benchmark.',
 qcRouterStart:'First year QC routing systems are installed. Each year adds the configured number until the fleet maximum.',
 qcRouterInitial:'Routing hardware installed in the first year. Annual deployment can keep some installed capacity uncommissioned while assignments migrate through 2040; actual migration cannot exceed installed capacity.',
 qcRouterAdd:'Adds QC ASSIGNMENT ROUTERS, independent of scientific tool QPUs. Purchase costs and power scale with installed units.',
 qcRouterMax:'Maximum installed QC routing systems. Coverage saturates at 100%; extra hardware can add overhead without benefit.',
 qcRouterCoverage:'ASSUMPTION: one QC router can manage this percentage of the 2030 assignment workload. It controls how much of the source policy opportunity is reached. No measured chip-to-workload conversion exists.',
 qcRouterGain:'ASSUMPTION: additional throughput on work covered by the QC rollout. 10% is an optimistic proposal input, not a published quantum advantage. Set 0% to remove it.',
 qcRouterMw:'PER ROUTER: system electricity for the assignment QC, independent of optional QPUs used for scientific tool jobs. The sourced proposal uses 0.0125 MW from the Advantage2 vendor report as a hardware proxy; external host overhead is excluded unless entered as support power.',
 qcRouterPrice:'Illustrative QC router purchase price, including its internal controls and cooling. The proposal uses the $20M FAU system agreement as a transaction proxy, not a universal list price or a 2030 quote.',
 qcRouterLatencyMs:'Time to obtain an assignment; a hypothetical parameter. A faster decision does not automatically improve placement quality.',
 qGrowth:'Annual compound gain applied to both quantum jobs/hour and logical qubits for NEW purchases. These are a simplified shared growth assumption.',
 qMw:'Complete quantum system wall power, including refrigeration and internal controls. It is not heat deposited in the qubit chip.',
 qPowerDrop:'Annual compound decline in wall power of NEW purchases. Existing quantum systems keep their purchase-year power.',
 specDecode:'Use the existing fleet model’s explicit assumption of two accepted tokens per step; not a measured quantum effect.',
 coolingCapex:'Cooling installation price per kW. The research notes do not supply this cost; blank means unknown.',
 qPrice:'Purchase price of one complete quantum system, in millions of dollars. Imported prices remain assumptions, not vendor quotes.',
 qPriceDrop:'Annual compound decline in the price of NEW quantum purchases. It does not refund older purchases.',
 router:'Classical baseline or a hypothetical quantum-assisted scheduler. The repository’s greedy and batch policies themselves are classical algorithms.',
 routerGain:'Explicit assumed scheduling throughput gain. Defaults to zero; it is not derived from energy savings. Quantum scheduling reserves one capable system.',
 routerMs:'Assumed time to make an assignment. The 100 ms check is a chosen modeling limit, not an official challenge deadline.',
 routerKw:'Extra classical support electricity for hypothetical quantum-assisted scheduling, in addition to the quantum system.',
 cooling:'Cooling technology and its modeled rack-density limit, facility overhead and installation cost.',
 climate:'Cold or desert site assumptions change facility cooling overhead. The site view distributes capacity equally rather than optimizing real climates.',
 coolant:'Temperature of liquid supplied to the chip cooling system. Lower temperature can help chip temperature, but this proxy does not calculate a chiller cycle.',
 resistance:'ASSUMPTION, not measured: chip-to-coolant thermal resistance describes how much chip temperature rises per watt. Example: 5,500 W × 0.009 K/W = 49.5°C rise.',
 tempLimit:'Assumed maximum chip temperature. This needs a validated hardware specification.',
 util:'Annual average GPU utilization used for energy estimation. Installed idle power still consumes electricity.',
 electricity:'Price per kWh. Cost can fall by using cheaper sites even when total energy does not fall.',
 building:'Building and power infrastructure cost per installed kW, in thousands of dollars, charged on new equipment.',
 network:'Extra networking purchase cost as a percentage of new GPU hardware cost.',
 maintenance:'Annual maintenance cost as a percentage of accumulated installed capital.',
 prefill:'Extra GPU-power allowance for prefill and memory work missing from the decode-only base model. It is not detailed capacity sizing.',
 otherMw:'Fixed classical CPU, storage and support power. Quantum computers do not replace these general functions.'
};
$('control-guide').innerHTML=groups.map(([group,fields])=>`<h3>${group}</h3><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>${fields.map(([id,label])=>`<tr><th>${label}</th><td>${CONTROL_HELP[id]}</td></tr>`).join('')}</tbody></table>`).join('');

$('evidence-register').innerHTML=`<div class="table-wrap"><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Input</th><th>Value / classification</th><th>Evidence and limits</th></tr></thead><tbody>${Object.entries(EVIDENCE).map(([id,e])=>`<tr><th>${id}</th><td>${e.value}<br><small>${e.kind}</small></td><td>${e.url?`<a href="${e.url}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">${e.title}</a><br>`:''}${e.note}</td></tr>`).join('')}</tbody></table></div><p>Evidence checked 18 October 2026. Editing an input replaces its proxy with your assumption; source links do not validate edited values. No automatic 2030–2040 advance is implied.</p>`;
